Example: You get 400 a month. You plan 150 for food, 60 for transport, 40 for phone and bills, 50 for fun, 50 for study materials and 50 for savings. That adds up to 400, so every unit has a job.

Tip: Review your budget at the end of each month. Move money from categories you did not use to the ones that ran short, and try again next month.', 'Beginner', 'education', 1);

• 50% for needs – food, rent, transport, bills.
• 30% for wants – entertainment, eating out, shopping.
• 20% for savings and paying back any debt.

Students often have fewer fixed bills, so you might save more than 20%. Treat the rule as a guide, not a law – adjust it to your own situation.', 'Beginner', 'bills', 1);

Example: When your allowance arrives, put 10% straight into your savings goal in PennyPal. Then budget the other 90%.

Small amounts add up: saving 5 a day becomes about 150 a month and 1,800 a year.', 'Beginner', 'savings', 1);

Aim for one month of expenses first, then grow it to three months. Keep it separate from your spending money so you are not tempted to use it.

Create a savings goal called "Emergency fund" in PennyPal and add a small monthly contribution.', 'Intermediate', 'savings', 1);

Some income is regular (a monthly allowance) and some is irregular (freelance work, gifts). Build your budget on the regular income, and treat irregular income as a bonus – save part of it.

Record every income entry in PennyPal so your available balance is always accurate.', 'Beginner', 'gift', 1);

Optional expenses (wants) make life nicer but can be reduced: takeaway coffee, new clothes, streaming services, concerts.

Before buying, ask: "Do I need this, or do I want it?" If it is a want, wait 24 hours. If you still want it and it fits your budget, go ahead.', 'Beginner', 'shopping', 1);

Use the Transaction History filter in PennyPal to view one category for the month. If Food shows many small entries, try packing lunch twice a week and compare the next month.', 'Intermediate', 'food', 1);

Make a list at the start of the term, estimate the total and divide it by the months until you need the money. Add that amount to your Education budget each month.

Look for second-hand books, library copies and student discounts before paying full price.', 'Beginner', 'education', 1);

